  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in\nmany areas tonight, Saturday night, and Sunday night.\n\nWith a large area of Canadian high pressure building overhead\nthrough the weekend, conditions will likely become favorable \nfor radiational cooling (light to calm winds, mainly clear skies\nand a cool background airmass). At the moment it is unclear\nwhich night will be the coldest, although Saturday night will\nhave a slight advantage given the position of the high.\n\nThe growing season for 2026 has ended along and west of the Blue\nRidge and Catoctin Mountains, but continues east of there. Any\nheadlines for frost/freeze conditions will only be issued where\nthe growing season remains active.\n\nFor tonight, temperatures look to fall into the mid to upper 30s\nfor many areas (slightly colder west of the Blue Ridge and\nslightly warmer in the urban areas and bayshore). The typically\ncolder spots of northern MD and the central VA piedmont could \nat least briefly come close to freezing. For the afternoon \npackage, a Frost Advisory was only issued from Frederick to \nCecil Counties. These areas will be closer to the surface high \nand have lower surface dew points. Farther to the south, there \nwill be a background easterly flow and perhaps some residual \nclouds.\n\nFor Saturday night, more widespread cold (possible frost/freeze)\nconditions are possible as high pressure settles more directly \noverhead. This would bring the frost/freeze threat for most \nareas outside the major urban centers and immediate shore of the\nChesapeake Bay. If the colder temperature guidance verifies, \nthen the growing season may end for more areas west I-95 come \nearly Sunday morning. The high will remain overhead through \nMonday morning, and the low level airmass doesn't change \nappreciably, so frost/freeze conditions will be possible again \nSunday night.\n\nDid not make appreciable changes to the previous min temperature\nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10 percentile and\nCanadian Regional).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues through next week along\nwith generally seasonable temperatures.\n\nCanadian high pressure will continue to prevail overhead over the \nnext several days as upper-level ridging builds in from the west. As \nthis ridging tracks east, a dry backdoor cold front will move \nthrough Tuesday and into Wednesday, with winds shifting \nnortheasterly in the wake of the front. With PWATs remaining dry \nthrough this period, no precipitation is anticipated as this front \nmoves through. \n\nAs this surface high pressure continues on, temperatures remain \nseasonable, with highs in the 60s to low-70s. Overnight lows could\ngradually increase somewhat throughout the week as this high \npressure persists, with skies remaining mostly clear throughout the \nweek. A few models hint at this high pressure breaking down towards \nthe end of next week as a weak trough begins to form over the Ohio \nRiver Valley next Thursday. Regardless, expect continued dry & \nseasonable conditions to continue at least through the next several \ndays as the current pattern remains largely unchanged.",
